+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+The repository has separate cart, order, catalog inventory, and REST layers. Product quantity is represented by the product's availability/inventory quantity and is exposed through product reads. Existing order processing already contains inventory-related behavior, while cart and product availability paths need to share the same stock semantics.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Centralize stock lookup, validation, decrement, and restoration by product SKU/identity.
+- Make checkout and multi-item cart validation all-or-nothing within the existing service transaction boundaries.
+- Make cancellation restoration idempotent through the persisted order status transition.
+- Map stock conflicts to HTTP 409 consistently across public and authenticated endpoints.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Redesigning product or order persistence schemas.
+- Introducing reservations that expire before checkout.
+- Changing merchant availability controls or order pricing rules.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Use the product associated with each cart/order line as the sole inventory target. This avoids positional or shared-object updates that can decrement another product.
+- Validate every line before mutating stock or creating an order. For multi-item cart changes, validate a prospective copy of the cart first, then persist once all lines pass.
+- Keep stock mutation in the existing catalog inventory/product service and invoke it from transactional cart/order workflows. Reusing service-level persistence avoids duplicate quantity semantics.
+- Treat the CANCELED status transition as the restoration event. Guard transitions from CANCELED and perform restoration only when the prior status was not CANCELED, making repeated cancellation a no-op.
+- Compute API availability as merchant availability AND positive stock, and compute purchasability with the same stock gate. This preserves explicit merchant disablement while removing zero-stock products from sale.
+- Represent failures with the project's service/business exception path and extend REST exception mapping to 409 rather than leaking partial updates.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Concurrent checkouts can race on stock] → use transaction boundaries and repository updates/locking appropriate to the existing persistence layer, with validation and decrement in one transaction.
+- [Legacy orders may already be CANCELED without a restoration marker] → rely on the status transition guard for new operations and avoid retroactive inventory changes.
+- [Existing callers may expect cart service exceptions] → preserve service contracts and change only the REST mapping/status for stock conflicts.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Deploy the service and controller changes together, then run the targeted cart, checkout, order-status, and product integration tests. No data migration is required; rollback is code rollback, with any already-applied stock changes requiring normal operational reconciliation.
