+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Product quantity is the stock value exposed by the product API, but cart and order flows do not consistently validate or mutate that value. This permits overselling, decrements the wrong product, fails to restore canceled orders, and reports out-of-stock products as purchasable.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Make checkout atomically validate every cart line and decrement exactly the matching product's stock.
+- Reject checkout and cart mutations with HTTP 409 when requested quantities exceed stock, preserving the prior cart, order, and inventory state.
+- Apply the same stock checks to single-item, multi-item, and new-cart additions; always permit reductions and removals.
+- Restore each canceled order line to its product stock exactly once, and reject transitions away from an already canceled order.
+- Derive product API `available` and `canBePurchased` from stock while preserving the merchant's explicit unavailable setting.
+- Add regression coverage for product identity, atomicity, status transitions, cart validation, and availability.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+- `stock-inventory`: Consistent stock validation, reservation, restoration, and availability behavior across carts, checkout, orders, and product reads.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+None.
+
+## Impact
+
+Affected areas include the core catalog inventory and order services, shopping-cart services, REST cart/order/product controllers and exception mapping, product availability population, persistence transaction boundaries, and integration/unit tests across `sm-core`, `sm-shop`, and model modules. The public APIs gain the specified HTTP 409 conflict behavior.
